Z||000000||William||04-02-2000||10:47 AM||||I just finished painted my trim and all looks excellent, however, the masking tape on the rear appears to have gotten hot and when removed left 30% of the adhesive on the car !!!!!!!!!! I'm staring to kind of freak out here. The paint job is only about 1 month old and this stuff WON'T come off. I've tried windex ond soapy water. How can I removed this without hurting the clearcoat?????||||reg||8
Z||000001||2M4 Dale||04-02-2000||11:01 AM||||Try one of those citrus cleaners, like De-Solve-It. Do it in an un-obvious place first. I don't think it will hurt your paint. ||||reg||3
Z||000002||William||04-02-2000||11:51 AM||||Update,<BR>My wise and wonderful granfather recommended lighter fluid. Ouch, well it couldn't be any worse then the mess I HAD! I gave it a try and yep, it all instantly came off. I quicky soaped it all down and gave it a rinse. The paint seems fine and it's all still glossy.<BR>I'm slowly learning that no matter how many nice tools I buy and even with alot of researh and preparation, I'm just not a car guy. As much as I would like to do a kit Lambo project, I begining to think that it would be a lost cause. What a shame... :-(||||reg||1
Z||000003||DXR_DAD||04-02-2000||12:59 PM||||3M makes a bumper sticker/adhesive remover. Don't have it at hand to get the 3M part# but it works, I used it when I did my sail panels As William said: RINSE WELL!! ||||reg||14
Z||000004||PAUL88GT||04-03-2000||06:07 PM||||I generally use a soft cloth with isopropyl alcohol (rubbing alcohol) to remove adhesives left from decals and tape. It does not seem to harm the clear coat.||||reg||1
